emitSearchTraceLimit ranks traces by `min(Timestamp) DESC, TraceId ASC` and
keeps the first N. The TraceId leg is not cosmetic: when two traces share a
start time it decides which one the page keeps, so at the LIMIT boundary a
wrong direction is not a reordering — it is a trace the user should have seen
going missing.

This fixture puts the tie exactly on that boundary. `search_limit: 2` over
three traces, all matching, all inside the request window:

  - `ffff…ff03` starts at 00:00:30 — strictly newest, takes slot 1 outright.
  - `0000…aa01` and `0000…bb02` BOTH start at 00:00:10 — an exact tie on
    min(Timestamp), so the sort key alone cannot separate them. They straddle
    slot 2: one is inside the page, one is outside, and only the TraceId leg
    decides which.

ASC picks `0000…aa01`, so the outer drain returns that trace's two spans plus
the newest trace's span; `0000…bb02` contributes nothing. Flip the tie-break
to DESC and the expected rows become `0000…bb02`'s — the fixture fails. The
seed also discriminates the sort key itself: `0000…bb02` has the later LAST
span (00:00:25 vs 00:00:20), so ranking by max(Timestamp) instead of min lands
on the other side of the boundary too, and ranking ASC instead of DESC drops
the newest trace.

The matcher is the `name` intrinsic rather than a resource attribute so the
seed carries no Map column — this fixture's purpose is pinning the TraceId
tie-break order, and staying Map-free keeps it that way rather than also
depending on the Map round-trip. That combination WAS a forced choice: the
round-trip harness could not enumerate a bare `SELECT * FROM <table>` to wrap
a Map in toJSONString, so no fixture of this drain shape could assert on a
Map column at all. Resolved in this change (#1431) — expandQualifiedStar now
falls back to the fixture's own seed DDL for a bare table scan's column list,
and search_trace_limit_bare_table_map_column.txtar exercises the Map-bearing
case directly.

-- search_limit --
2
-- search_window --
1767225600 1767225660
-- query.traceql --
{ name = "checkout" }
-- seed --
CREATE TABLE otel_traces (
    TraceId String,
    SpanId String,
    Timestamp DateTime64(9),
    SpanName String
) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY (Timestamp, SpanId);
INSERT INTO otel_traces VALUES
    ('0000000000000000000000000000aa01', '0000000000000011', toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:10', 9), 'checkout'),
    ('0000000000000000000000000000aa01', '0000000000000012', toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:20', 9), 'checkout'),
    ('0000000000000000000000000000bb02', '0000000000000021', toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:10', 9), 'checkout'),
    ('0000000000000000000000000000bb02', '0000000000000022', toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:25', 9), 'checkout'),
    ('ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff03', '0000000000000031', toDateTime64('2026-01-01 00:00:30', 9), 'checkout');
-- expected_rows --
[
  ["checkout", {"__cerberus_parentSpanID": "", "__cerberus_spanID": "0000000000000011", "__cerberus_traceID": "0000000000000000000000000000aa01"}, "2026-01-01T00:00:10Z", 0],
  ["checkout", {"__cerberus_parentSpanID": "", "__cerberus_spanID": "0000000000000012", "__cerberus_traceID": "0000000000000000000000000000aa01"}, "2026-01-01T00:00:20Z", 0],
  ["checkout", {"__cerberus_parentSpanID": "", "__cerberus_spanID": "0000000000000031", "__cerberus_traceID": "ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff03"}, "2026-01-01T00:00:30Z", 0]
]
-- args --
[0] string = "checkout"
[1] int64 = 1767225600000000000
[2] int64 = 1767225660000000000
[3] string = "checkout"
[4] int64 = 1767225600000000000
[5] int64 = 1767225660000000000
-- chplan --
SearchTraceLimit traceLimit=2
  Filter predicate=(((SpanName = "checkout") AND (Timestamp >= FnFromUnixNanos(1767225600000000000))) AND (Timestamp <= FnFromUnixNanos(1767225660000000000)))
    Scan(otel_traces)
-- sql --
SELECT s.* FROM (SELECT * FROM `otel_traces` PREWHERE (`SpanName` = ?) WHERE (`Timestamp` >= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?)) AND (`Timestamp` <= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?))) AS s WHERE `TraceId` GLOBAL IN (SELECT `TraceId` FROM (SELECT * FROM `otel_traces` PREWHERE (`SpanName` = ?) WHERE (`Timestamp` >= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?)) AND (`Timestamp` <= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?))) GROUP BY `TraceId` ORDER BY min(`Timestamp`) DESC, `TraceId` LIMIT 2)
-- parity_exempt --
reason: reference-fetch-layer
detail: `{ name = "checkout" }` carries `search_window:`, which narrows cerberus's scan where upstream narrows in its FETCH layer before the pipeline the oracle runs
-- args_optimized --
[0] string = "__cerberus_traceID"
[1] string = "__cerberus_parentSpanID"
[2] string = "__cerberus_spanID"
[3] string = "checkout"
[4] int64 = 1767225600000000000
[5] int64 = 1767225660000000000
[6] string = "checkout"
[7] int64 = 1767225600000000000
[8] int64 = 1767225660000000000
-- settings --
enable_analyzer=1
enabled_opts=aggregation_in_order,condition_cache,exp_histogram_two_level,join_spill,lazy_materialization,result_cache,trace_id_bitmap_filter
log_comment=cerb:project
max_bytes_before_external_group_by=536870912
max_bytes_before_external_sort=536870912
use_query_condition_cache=1
-- sql_optimized --
SELECT `SpanName` AS `MetricName`, mapConcat(`ResourceAttributes`, map(?, `TraceId`, ?, `ParentSpanId`, ?, `SpanId`)) AS `Attributes`, `Timestamp` AS `TimeUnix`, toFloat64(`Duration`) AS `Value` FROM (SELECT s.* FROM (SELECT * FROM `otel_traces` PREWHERE (`SpanName` = ?) WHERE (`Timestamp` >= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?)) AND (`Timestamp` <= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?))) AS s WHERE `TraceId` GLOBAL IN (SELECT `TraceId` FROM (SELECT * FROM `otel_traces` PREWHERE (`SpanName` = ?) WHERE (`Timestamp` >= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?)) AND (`Timestamp` <= fromUnixTimestamp64Nano(?))) GROUP BY `TraceId` ORDER BY min(`Timestamp`) DESC, `TraceId` LIMIT 2))
